# The `between` references that land on a layer this corpus holds. One row per resolved
# reference, carrying the filing the notation resolves to.
#
# The references that land, named
#   The ones that do not land are the interesting ones, and they are found as everything in
#   `eliminations/references.sqlc` that is not here: the references that point outside what is
#   here, which the schema calls ordinary rather than wrong. In a model about typed blanks, the
#   set that lands is the one worth naming.
#
# Resolution rests on what a document says about itself
#   As it does for a part: `filing_identity` is what a document says about itself, and without it
#   a reference to another document cannot be resolved from the corpus at all.
#
# `pm.layer` directly, not `layers/every_layer.sqlc`
#   The last join only asks whether the layer this reference names exists.
#   `layers/every_layer.sqlc` lists every layer and is kept for denominators. Composed here, it
#   would end up among the relations most of the checks compose, and any law asking what a rule
#   reaches would then hold without testing anything, because that list reaches everything.
#   `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc` states the rule and enforces it over what every relation composes.
-- eliminations/references.sqlc joined through pm.filing_identity to pm.layer.
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.quantity, b.seq,
       b.party, b.notation, fi.filing AS resolved_filing, b.layer, b.regime
FROM      (
    :compose(eliminations/references.sqlc)
) b
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/notations.sqlc)
) fi ON fi.notation = b.notation
JOIN pm.layer l  ON l.filing = fi.filing AND l.layer = b.layer
